    "result_text": "The build passes and the acceptance test is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI unified `customerCode` and `membershipNumber` into a single `memberId` formatted `<REGION><FY><HASH8><CHK>`, and removed the standalone `checkDigit`. Verified by the audit output `memberId=NSW2616E6609E3` = `NSW` + FY `26` + HASH8 `16E6609E` + Luhn CHK `3`.\n\n**Design for low structural impact.** The entity already stores whatever `CustomerCode.of()` returns, dedups it via `CustomerCodes.uniqueFor()`, and exposes it via `getCustomerCode()`. So I repurposed the *small* `CustomerCode.of()` to emit the full `memberId`, which let me leave the two largest classes \u2014 `Owner` and `OwnerRestControllerV1` \u2014 and the collision helper `CustomerCodes` **completely untouched**. Collision handling, the audit record, and the owner segment all now reference the `memberId` for free because they read through that same identifier.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `CustomerCode.java` \u2014 `of()` now builds `<REGION><FY><HASH8><CHK>` (added `region()` for reuse, private `hash8()`/`luhn()`).\n- `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 dropped the `membershipNumber` and `checkDigit` mappings, added `memberId`, and rederived `locality`/`timezone` from `CustomerCode.region(owner)` (the old code split the id on `-`, which the delimiter-free `memberId` no longer has).\n- `OwnerSegments.java` \u2014 region now via `CustomerCode.region(owner)`.\n- `OwnerAuditListener.java` / `OwnerCreatedEvent.java` \u2014 log the `memberId` (dropped the dead `membershipNumber` helper).\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 replaced the three DTO fields with a single `memberId`.\n- Deleted `CheckDigits.java` (its only caller, the `checkDigit` mapping, is gone).\n\n**Notes worth flagging:**\n- I intentionally kept the internal entity field/getter named `customerCode` and the helper class names `CustomerCode`/`CustomerCodes`. Renaming them would force edits into the large `Owner` class and the controller (whose surrounding complexity dominates the structural-impact cost) for no behavioural gain \u2014 the exposed API field is correctly `memberId`, which is what the spec and tests assert. If you'd prefer the internal names renamed too for clarity, I can do that, but it raises structural impact.\n- The `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` DB column is untouched and comfortably fits the new id (14\u201318 chars for the region cases tests exercise).",
